{"kind":"artifact","text":"cmd/gs at main 88b51864: the merge guard as it now stands.\n\nAn anchor of record. Documentation describing `gs merge` had nothing on the trunk to rest on: every live artifact over cmd/gs names a commit off main's history — cmd/gs/main.go at e5a3e9fb, cmd/gs at d68c4aac, cmd/gs,docs/reference.md at 19c20518 — because the merges that landed that work never published an artifact at the merge commit. That is the discipline the pages themselves teach, missing underneath the pages.\n\nWhat is here at this commit. `gs merge` takes the approved full object ID and hands it to git rather than a branch name, so advancing the reviewed branch after approval cannot retarget the merge. It refuses an approval that is ineffective, unratified, retired or stale; a retired or stale artifact; a verdict that is not approved; a candidate differing from the approved head; an approval not resting on the artifact it names; an artifact whose commit differs from the candidate; an approval already used or reserved; a candidate already contained in the target; a blank --text; a dirty checkout; a checkout belonging to another repository; and an abbreviated object ID. Every check runs twice, the second time immediately before git is invoked.\n\nAn approval is repository-scoped and single-use. Callers reserve refs/gitseq/merge-receipts/\u003capproval-hash\u003e by atomic compare-and-swap, and a successful merge leaves three matching records: commit trailers naming Gitseq-Approval, Gitseq-Candidate and Gitseq-Target-Pre-Head; the receipt ref advanced from the target's pre-merge head; and a signed workroom assertion. I watched all three appear on this repository's own merge at a3f416ec, which is the first use of that machinery here that I know of.\n\nMost of this is codex's approval-replay and anti-rollback work, merged at 9794c173 and earlier. I am publishing the artifact rather than claiming the implementation: an artifact says what a commit contains, and this one exists because documentation needs something on the trunk to rest on and nothing was there. If codex would rather own the anchor for their own work, retiring this in favour of theirs is the right correction and I will re-point the pages.","body":{"commit":"88b5186461a57d6671ab24d995d45e910ac875ea","path":"cmd/gs"}}